Answer the following multiple choice question. The entire content of your response should be of the following format: 'ANSWER: $LETTER' (without quotes) where LETTER is one of {letters}.

{question}

{choices}

Instruction: Read the segment of a merger agreement and answer the multiple-choice question by choosing the option that best characterizes the agreement.\nQuestion: Is \u201cfinancial point of view\u201d the sole consideration when determining whether an offer is superior?\nOption A: No\nOption B: Yes\n\nMerger Agreement: SECTION 9.2. Consideration of Proposals. <omitted> (b) Notwithstanding anything in this Agreement to the contrary, prior to obtaining the Requisite Shareholder Approval, the Company Board may make a Company Recommendation Change <omitted> if, and only if, <omitted> (iii) the Company Board determines in good faith, after consultation with the Company’s outside legal counsel and a financial advisor of nationally recognized reputation, that the failure to make such a change would be inconsistent with the fiduciary duties of the Company Board under applicable Law, (iv) the Company has provided Parent with written notice of the Company Board’s intention to make a Company Recommendation Change or terminate this Agreement to enter into a definitive agreement with respect to a Superior Proposal, and (v) the Company Board has given due consideration to any adjustments proposed by Parent to the terms of this Agreement, and after such consideration, determines in good faith that the Superior Proposal continues to be superior, taking into account all aspects of the proposal, including legal, financial, and reputational factors. For clarity, any amendment to the financial terms or other material amendments to any Acquisition Proposal shall be deemed a new proposal, requiring a new notice period of three Business Days. (Pages 88-89)\nAnswer:
Choices:

Answer the following multiple choice question. The entire content of your response should be of the following format: 'ANSWER: $LETTER' (without quotes) where LETTER is one of {letters}.

{question}

{choices}

Instruction: Read the segment of a merger agreement and answer the multiple-choice question by choosing the option that best characterizes the agreement.\nQuestion: Is \u201cfinancial point of view\u201d the sole consideration when determining whether an offer is superior?\nOption A: No\nOption B: Yes\n\nMerger Agreement: SECTION 9.3. Recommendations and Offers. <omitted> (c) Notwithstanding anything in this Agreement to the contrary, during the Pre-Closing Period, the Company Board may change its recommendation <omitted> if, and only if, <omitted> (ii) the Company Board determines in good faith, after consultation with the Company\u2019s outside legal counsel and considering its fiduciary duties under applicable Law, that the failure to change its recommendation would be inconsistent with its fiduciary duties, (iii) the Company has provided Parent with written notice of the Company Board\u2019s intention to change its recommendation not earlier than 11:59 p.m. New York time on the fifth Business Day after Parent receives such written notice and (iv) if not in connection with an Intervening Event pursuant to Section 9.3(e), the decision to change its recommendation is based on an Acquisition Proposal that the Company Board has determined to be a Superior Proposal, and the Company shall have complied with clauses (1) through (5), as follows: (1) prior to giving effect to clauses (2) through (5), the Company Board shall have determined that such Acquisition Proposal is a Superior Proposal, (2) the Company shall have made available to Parent the material terms and conditions of such Acquisition Proposal and copies of all written communications relating to such Acquisition Proposal in accordance with Section 9.3(b), (3) the Company shall have negotiated in good faith with Parent during the five Business Day period provided in the foregoing clause (iii) of this Section 9.3(c) with respect to such proposed revisions to this Agreement or other proposals made by Parent, if any, so that the Acquisition Proposal would no longer constitute a Superior Proposal, (4) after considering the results of negotiations with Parent and taking into account the proposals made by Parent, if any, after consultation with its outside legal counsel and a financial advisor of nationally recognized reputation, the Company Board shall have determined in good faith that such Acquisition Proposal remains a Superior Proposal, and, after consultation with its outside legal counsel, that the failure to make the recommendation change would be inconsistent with the fiduciary duties of the Company Board under applicable Law and (5) if the Company intends to terminate this Agreement to enter into a Specified Agreement, the Company shall have complied with Section 10.1(d)(i). For clarity, the provisions of this Section 9.3(c) shall also apply to any amendment to the financial terms or any other material amendment to any Acquisition Proposal (except that any reference to five Business Days shall instead be three Business Days) or any successive Acquisition Proposals. (Pages 80-81)\nAnswer:
Choices:

